# id: BM14-drop-the-lock
# blinds: the DB mutex on the write path
# covering-lane: cpp-tsan
# expect: killed
# control-lane: cpp-test
# expect-control: alive
#
# The catalogue's BM14. This is what proves the cpp-tsan lane is not decoration:
# a TSan lane over single-threaded tests is a green lane that proves nothing,
# and until this patch has been seen to redden it, green there is
# indistinguishable from a lane with no threads in it.
#
# The control is cpp-test, which BUILDS the harness and deliberately does not
# RUN it, so the patch is shown to compile and the red under TSan is the race
# rather than a broken build. Running the harness under cpp-test would redden it
# too -- an unlocked skiplist corrupts under four threads often enough -- and
# there would be no control.
#
# B1-D6c is why this matters more than a normal lock bug. The ruling chose a
# mutex over a lock-free skiplist precisely because the lock-free failure mode
# is "a bug that appears on one machine, at one core count, one run in ten
# thousand, and does not replay". Removing the mutex reintroduces exactly that
# failure mode, and TSan is the only instrument in this project that sees it.
--- a/engine-cpp/src/memtable/memtable.cc	2026-08-24 19:37:57
+++ b/engine-cpp/src/memtable/memtable.cc	2026-08-24 19:37:57
@@ -101,7 +101,7 @@
   const uint32_t ikey_len = static_cast<uint32_t>(user_key.size() + 8);
   const std::size_t bytes = 4 + ikey_len + 4 + value.size();
 
-  std::lock_guard<std::mutex> guard(mu_);
+  // BM14: the write path no longer takes the lock
 
   if (head_ == nullptr) {
     // The head has no entry and full height; nothing ever compares against it.
